<h2>The question</h2>

<p><a href="/backlog/3658-self-registering-provider-descriptors-for-dispatch-provider/">#3658</a> proposes
that mechanical-dispatch providers register themselves through descriptor files instead of being
hand-edited into one shared table, so that adding an Antigravity or Codex provider does not collide with
other lanes. This survey tests that claim against the code and against how other plugin systems do it. The
full working is in <a href="/reports/2026-09-21-provider-registration-grounding.md">the session report</a>.
Prototype-branch files were read at <code>origin/lane/mechanical-dispatcher</code> <code>13affbab1</code>;
main at <code>18798aec3</code>.</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — the card names the wrong table</h2>

<p><code>DISPATCH_PROVIDER_REGISTRY</code> (prototype branch,
<code>scripts/operations/dispatch-provider-registry.mjs:77</code>) is keyed by <strong>launch kind</strong>:
<code>build</code>, <code>prepare</code>, <code>fix</code>, <code>prepare-decision</code>,
<code>ci-heal</code>. Its keys must be members of <code>LAUNCH_KINDS</code> (six members,
<code>dispatch-lane.mjs:188</code>), checked when the module loads. Five kinds have a row; one more
(<code>investigate</code>) can be added, and then the table is full.</p>

<p>That table already fixed the collision the card describes. Before it, five lanes each added an
<code>if (kind === …)</code> arm to one router in a 2000-line file. After the extraction on 2026-09-12, the
four sibling kinds landed the same day as one import and one row each.</p>

<h2>Finding 2 — the real collision surface is the vendor axis</h2>

<p>A new vendor is the command-line tool that runs the agent. It is not a row in the kind table. On the
prototype branch, adding one means editing:</p>

<ul>
<li><code>DELIVERY_AGENT_PROVIDERS</code> in <code>deliver-item-wrapper.mjs</code> (1856 lines), plus a
vendor object of about 90 lines;</li>
<li><code>FIX_AGENT_PROVIDERS</code> in <code>fix-dispatch-wrapper.mjs</code> (852 lines), plus its vendor
object;</li>
<li><code>CI_HEAL_AGENT_PROVIDERS</code> in <code>ci-heal-dispatch-wrapper.mjs</code> (862 lines), plus its
vendor object;</li>
<li><code>PROVIDERS</code>, <code>EXECUTOR_PROVIDERS</code> and <code>EXECUTABLE_PROVIDER</code> in
<code>scripts/lib/dispatch-contracts.mjs</code>;</li>
<li>and the router's cascade names vendors inline (<code>scripts/lib/provider-routing.mjs:423</code>,
<code>:452</code>, <code>:500-512</code>).</li>
</ul>

<p>So the card's worry is real, and wider than it says. It is just aimed at the wrong file.</p>

<h2>Finding 3 — the three vendor tables are forced equal, so a vendor cannot skip a kind</h2>

<p>The wrapper tests assert that the fix and ci-heal tables hold exactly the build table's vendor names. So
a vendor added to one table only fails CI. The consequence is the opposite of drift: every vendor must cover
every kind. "Antigravity runs build and fix but not ci-heal" cannot be stated. And one
<code>deliveryAgent:</code> marker on an item applies to all three kinds, so once a vendor may lack a kind,
the marker needs a rule for that case.</p>

<h2>Finding 4 — what main has</h2>

<p>Main has the <code>provider</code> port (#3579) with one implementation, <code>defaultClaudeProvider</code>
(<code>scripts/operations/dispatch-lane-io.mjs:1016</code>). It also has a
<code>DELIVERY_AGENT_PROVIDERS</code> table whose <code>codex</code> entry is a stub that throws. The kind
registry, the fix and ci-heal vendor tables, the real Codex delivery provider and
<code>dispatch-contracts.mjs</code> exist only on the prototype branch.</p>

<h2>Prior art</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>System</th><th>How a plugin registers</th><th>Who decides trust / order</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Python entry points</td><td>manifest (<code>entry_points.txt</code>) found without importing the plugin</td><td>the host; name clashes are "the consumer's" to resolve</td></tr>
<tr><td>VS Code extensions</td><td>declarative <code>contributes</code> manifest; code loads on activation</td><td>the marketplace and the user install</td></tr>
<tr><td>Java <code>ServiceLoader</code></td><td><code>META-INF/services</code> manifest; bad entry raises an error during iteration</td><td>the caller iterating</td></tr>
<tr><td>Go <code>database/sql</code></td><td><code>init()</code> side effect of an explicit import; duplicate name panics</td><td>the caller names the driver</td></tr>
<tr><td>ESLint flat config</td><td>explicit imported plugin objects — replaced name-based discovery</td><td>the config author</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vite / Rollup</td><td>explicit plugin array</td><td>array order in the config</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vercel AI SDK</td><td><code>createProviderRegistry({ anthropic, openai })</code>, ids <code>providerId:modelId</code></td><td>the caller picks the model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backstage backend</td><td>explicit <code>backend.add(import(…))</code>; discovery is opt-in</td><td>the app</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two patterns hold across the survey. <strong>Discovery</strong> is used where third parties install
plugins the host cannot know about. Where one team owns every plugin in one repo, the trend is toward
<strong>explicit imports</strong>; ESLint's own post calls its custom name-based resolution "one of our
biggest regrets". And <strong>no surveyed system lets a plugin declare its own trust or its own
priority</strong>: that stays with the host.</p>

<h2>In-repo precedent</h2>

<ul>
<li>One file per entry plus a glob loader is proven for <em>data</em>: research topics (#1145), blocks
(#882), backlog items.</li>
<li>A static import plus a keyed table is the repo's pattern for <em>executable</em> registries:
<code>OPERATIONS</code> in <code>scripts/operations/run.mjs</code>, <code>constellation-repos.mjs</code>,
the kind registry itself.</li>
<li>The merge-risk statute
(<a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions/#merge-risk-optimistic-with-targeted-lock">#merge-risk-optimistic-with-targeted-lock</a>)
already classes a keyed table with distinct-key additions as safe for optimistic merge.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this reshapes</h2>

<p>"Self-registering vs hand-edited table" splits into four questions, each a fork on the card: which axis
the ruling governs (kind or vendor); how a vendor is collected (runtime discovery or an explicit index); what
a descriptor may declare (mechanics only: the kinds it can run, never trust or cascade order); and what an
item's vendor marker does when that vendor cannot run a kind. How a broken descriptor fails and where the
work is built turned out to be settled by precedent, not forks. <em>Corrected after the skeptic pass:</em>
the first draft said nothing checks the three tables agree; two tests do.</p>
